INT: Hello. It's April 16, 2024. The interviewee is Hal Block. I'm Andriette Redmann, the interviewer. And we're in Neptune, New Jersey. And the language will be English.

Hi. What is your name and your birthday and where were you born?

HB: I'm Hal Block. I was born January 21, 1932, in Gelsenkirchen, Germany.

INT: And your first language was?

HB: My first language was German.

INT: And your parents' names.

HB: My mother was Kate Strauss. And-- no, my mother's name was Kate Wolf. She later became Kate Strauss when my dad passed away. My father was Walter Ernest Block, a doctor in Gelsenkirchen.

INT: And when were they born and where?

HB: Ah, when were they born and where? My mother was born in Gelsenkirchen. And my father was born in-- and she was born in 1910. And I believe he was born in February of 1898 in Cologne, Germany.

INT: And were they religious?

HB: My mother's family were religious, so I don't believe my father's family were. We did practice some holidays, I'm told, while we were in Germany. But we did not participate in active religious practices when we were in the United States due to the fact that where we moved, it was a non-religious community. There were no temples, there were no Jews.

INT: And what did your dad do for a living?

HB: My father was a doctor. A general practitioner, they call it today.

INT: And was he ever in the military?

HB: No, he was not.

INT: His relatives were?

HB: My uncle, his brother, was in World War I as an aviator, in the Luftwaffe.

INT: And where was he born?

HB: He was also, as far as I know, born in Cologne. He later resided in Hartford, Connecticut.

INT: And was one in a prison camp?

HB: No, I had an uncle on my mother's side who was a-- in the US Army as an interpreter for German prisoners of war for the United states, you know, interviewing them on what they could find out about what the Nazis were doing, et cetera.

INT: And what were your parents like?

HB: They were wonderful. I mean, what were they like? My father was a strict German. He had a German temper. And he-- you did what you were told. And you were on time, always on time. If you weren't on time, you didn't get your dinner. You got sent to bed. My mother was wonderful. She adored me. I adored her. And she made me the person that I am today.

INT: And how did they meet?

HB: Well, I think she went for a physical examination to the local-- the local doctor in Gelsenkirchen, because that's where he established himself. And they met that way. And at that time, I believe they were still doing what they call matchmaking. And my grandparents wanted my mother to marry a professional.

INT: And did they go to the synagogue?

HB: I don't know.

INT: Or do you remember any holidays?

HB: I don't remember anything about-- I was three years old when I came over. I have probably zero memory of what happened in Germany, except for one incident, maybe. And that was my nursemaid took me to the park. That's all I remember about Germany.

INT: Do you have any memory of your house?

HB: No.

INT: Or any toys or food?

HB: The only toys I could remember is a scooter. And why it was never made and sold in the United States, I still would like to do it. It was a scooter, but it was chain driven. So you had-- you were pumping a pedal and it was-- you were propelling yourself. It was great. And also, the only other toy that I can remember that carried over was a train. They made wonderful trains. And they also had nice little soldiers and little things to go around the train tracks. That was nice.

INT: Any foods that you remember?

HB: No, no. I mean, the typical German food, you know, but nothing special.

INT: Did your mother keep cooking German style food in the US?

HB: My mother never cooked before.

INT: Oh, who was cooking?

HB: We-- both sides of the family were well to do. They both had nice businesses. And so they had help for me. They helped with the cooking, with everything. They did the washes. They did everything like that. So I-- she learned to cook because she had to.

INT: What else can you tell me that-- from the stories you've heard-- about Germany, like what life was like there?

HB: I really-- they did not talk too much about their experiences in Germany. Ours was something where we didn't hardly-- I don't speak hardly any German, only because they want to assimilate. They didn't want to dwell on the past. They only wanted to build a future in the United States. And so we didn't-- we did not get into anything other than some minor stories that I'm sharing some with you.

INT: Did they ever experience antisemitism back there?

HB: Well, yeah, when the Nazis were a party and the Brownshirts came around, my father had a very nice practice going. But it was sort of said that the non-Jewish doctor was a sort of jealous about this Jewish doctor who just came into town. And it was that they were coming for him one day, and they were going to drag-- the Brownshirts, the Nazis-- were going to drag him through the streets. His secretary, who was non-Jewish, informed him of this. And that's the time when my uncle arrived, took him-- at our house-- and took him to the local police station because you could give yourself up for safety reasons. And that's what happened. So we did experience antisemitism. And, of course, later on, the Nazis, you know, had basically when my mother was helping get cameras and stuff to the United States for my father to cash in and get his medical degree over here and have money for it, they did strip search her and stuff like that. But that's what we-- that's what I have understood to be.

INT: Where was when she was strip searched?

HB: She was on her way from Gelsenkirchen to Antwerp, where the ship was in which the purser-- there was a purser who was a friend of my father's who would take the Leicas, cameras, and take them to a store called Willoughby's in New York. And he would be paid money. And he would subsist on that and whatever else.

INT: Where did your mother get the cameras?

HB: Bought them. Yeah, again, we still had money when we were in Germany, so she was able to buy things.

INT: Did they have a sense that things were changing and that they'd need to leave? Do you know what--

HB: I guess when it came to drag him through the streets, that was enough. And--

INT: That was the first time?

HB: And things were kept getting worse. And so many people were very fortunate that my father-- that my father got over here because he brought in at least 10 or 12 people into this country and he sponsored them. And they all became very, when I look back at it, very productive citizens.

INT: Wonderful. And just curious about what kind of home did you live in? Was it a house or an apartment building? Or just curious about that.

HB: Well, I was looking for some pictures on that. And there was a place called the Cayuga Manor. That's where we first settled at Cayuga, New York.

INT: Oh, in New York.

HB: And that was a population of about 320 people. The main industry there was a milling company. And my father was to be the mill's doctor. That did not turn out to be. And that may be for-- I will not say what the reasons are, but I sort of surmise that they had something to do that we were German. I don't think that they even knew in that town what the word Jewish meant. But I think we were foreigners, and they didn't-- But we lived at the Cayuga Manor, which was a large, large boarding house. And he was-- it was big enough, evidently, for him to establish his practice there until such time as he-- we found-- he found a home, a house, which we rented for $18 a month. That was in 1936, '37. And he used one of the rooms in that house as a-- as an office. And my mother was his nurse. And then we lived there for, you know, till I was in fourth grade.

INT: That's good. So if you don't mind, if we could back up a little it.

HB: Sure.

INT: Because I want to hear the story of exactly how they came over. And before that, I think you mentioned you may have worn a star. Were you--

HB: I think there is some thought about the star. And I know that-- you know, when you're a child, you sort of mimic other kids. And everybody was saying Heil Hitler. And my mother said that I Heiled Hitler. And I was admonished for it because Jews were not allowed to Heil Hitler. That's one story that I do remember.

INT: And were there other kids that were Jewish around you? Other families?

HB: In the town that we moved? No.

INT: Yeah. That you were in Gelsenkirchen.

HB: Oh, In Gelsenkirchen, I don't know. I don't know. I know there are pictures of me with other children, but I assume there were. I don't know.

INT: OK. And then, so what was the journey that your parents made? I know your dad left first. And what happened--

HB: And my dad left. And we stayed in the Gelsenkirchen for-- I'm-- the timeline, I'm not 100% sure of. But in 1930-- in August of 1936, my mother and I were passengers on the SS Pennland. And we came to the United States.

INT: So was your dad-- he went before you did?

HB: Yeah, my dad went a couple of years before.

INT: What was his journey.

HB: Again, my timeline is not 100% right. But he left, I believe, in 1934. He was also a doctor on the Pennland. And so he traveled also between New York and Antwerp. Eventually, he migrated into Canada. And from Canada, he moved, migrated into the United States, where we were-- went-- ended up going to Cayuga, New York, when my mother and I came over. We lived there until I was in end of fourth grade, moved to Auburn, New York, where he also established a second practice and did very well there.

INT: Do you know why he went to Canada?

HB: I don't know. I don't know if it was easier to get into the United States. I don't know what it was. But that's--

INT: And how did he get the job on the ship?

HB: I have no idea.

INT: And then how many years was it until you followed with your mom?

HB: We came in '36.

INT: And then you ended up in Upstate New York.

HB: Yeah, Finger Lakes, in Cayuga.

INT: And is there a story about your dad saving a life?

HB: Well, my father was the ship's doctor on the Pennland. And there was a seaman on a tanker 650 miles away who was very ill, critically ill and dying. And they radioed the Pennland because they heard they were nearby. And he was the ship's doctor, of course. And between the two ships, radioed back and forth, he gave instructions on what they should be doing with this man and got his symptoms. And eventually, he saved his life. And he got-- he even got his picture in the New York Mirror and New York Post or whatever the newspaper was.

INT: That's great. And how did you get in? Something about the alien law, you were-- you had an exception?

HB: There is something. I'm trying to remember. In some document, it says something that we were-- or I and my mother swore allegiance to the United States of America. And in accordance with the law that was passed in 1940 something under the alien law, we were exempt from whatever that was. I'm not certain of that.

INT: And how was it adjusting to the United States? What do you know about it? I know you were very young. But what do you know about your parents getting used to it?

HB: It was very, very difficult for my mother. She couldn't speak the language. She'd never done any housework. She never was a nurse. She was never anything. And she-- my father had promised her that we would end up in Hempstead, New York, which ended up we weren't. We were-- he was not-- advised not to. There was a position available up in Cayuga, so that's where we ended up. It was very, very difficult time for her. I am assuming it was for him also. But you know, he got his practice started finally. And then he opened a second office in Auburn, New York. And so eventually, we moved to Auburn when I was in fifth grade. I adjusted very nicely in Cayuga. You know, there was at the time, the normal bullying, I guess you would call it today. And I was picked on. And the only thing was to do was you had to fight back if they, you know-- and I was engaged in many fights. And I guess once you let people know where you stand and they respect it and they leave you alone and you don't necessarily become their leader because they already, you know, have a leader. But I had good times. I-- at Cayuga, you'd go out into tracking rabbits or tracking muskrats. And what do I know? And swinging from vines and playing Tarzan. And I don't remember having a time-- a terrible time with the language. But I remember that the kids used to sit on the steps of the school. And I would be marching up and down in front of them and speaking. And they were all laughing because I didn't speak English, you know. I was talking to them in German, I guess. But I survived very well in that town. I had good teachers. It was a five-room schoolhouse. Nice thing was all grades, first and second, were taught in one-- with one teacher, third and fourth. And the nice part of it, if you were a little advanced, you got your second grade learning when you were in the first grade. So when I moved to Auburn and got into fifth grade, I was always already a little ahead.

INT: And did you-- were your parents at all religious at this point?

HB: There was not any-- if they were, they were in their hearts.

There wasn't-- there weren't any-- anything but Methodists and Catholics. No, they didn't have a Catholic church. I think it was a Methodist church and a Presbyterian church, and that was it. And so I wanted to assimilate-- I can't say I wanted to assimilate. What do I know about assimilating when I'm in second and third grade? I wanted to be-- have friends. So I went with my friends on Sunday. And where did my friends go? The Methodist church, so I learned a little about the Christian religion.

INT: How did that feel?

HB: Later on, it felt peculiar. At the time, it didn't. You'd say, you know, what the heck did I do? You know, I took part in all the ceremonies. I didn't know the difference.

INT: Did you ever celebrate any Christian holidays? Like back in Germany, did you have a Christmas tree? Did you mention that?

HB: Well, I don't remember the German part. But I know that when my grandmother came over, we-- that's my father's mother-- we-- she lived with us in that $18 house. And we had a Christmas tree. And I had my little train around there. And, yeah-- and I-- yeah, we did.

INT: How did they view the Christmas tree? Like, how did they see it?

HB: It's a Tannenbaum, you know. I don't think they-- we did not regard it as anything other than a beautiful tree with nice lights. And it was pretty. It didn't have any religious meaning to it at all. In fact-- yeah. Yeah.

INT: Did you have a bar mitzvah?

HB: I did not, NO. There was-- no, I did not.

INT: Did you have make any Jewish friends growing up at all?

HB: I had a lot of friends. I wouldn't-- I wouldn't say-- only because they were neighborhood people, because I was, again, not in that town, when we moved to Auburn, yeah, the Schwartzes and the [?Cowlitz?] and people like that, yes, I had Jewish friends. But again, it-- that was I think 32,000 people. It had a very small Jewish population. The friendship was not based on religion. It was based on we liked each other. And we went from there.

INT: So it was very mixed when you were--

HB: Yes.

INT: --growing up?

HB: Yeah.

INT: And where did you go to school?

HB: I went-- in high school, I went to a school called Auburn East High School.

INT: And what year--

HB: Graduated in 1950. I was president of my senior class. I enjoyed high school. I really had a good time. I think I was a bit of a social person and I had a lot of friends. And I was an organizer and did a lot of things within the school-- senior plays, all that kind of stuff.

INT: A leader. And then you went off to college, a few different colleges. Tell us about that.

HB: Well, I decided that I wanted to be a restaurant and hotel management. And University of Denver had just opened up a brand new school there in hotel management. And I got accepted there and went there. The unfortunate part is that the altitude didn't agree with me. And I found myself passing out. And after I think one or two semesters, I had to leave. I came home and worked a little and then got accepted at Temple University, where I stayed for a couple of years. And then I eventually came home and went to work for my uncle in Chicago as a salesperson.

INT: And there was-- was there City College also?

HB: Yes. Later on when I moved to New York, that was after going with my uncle, I decided perhaps, maybe-- or I didn't decide. My mother says, everyone's got to have a college education, you know. And so I went to City of New York night school for a bit of time. And then I ended up working harder and didn't have the time, or the night school hours.

INT: And when in this did your father pass away?

HB: My father passed away in 1953.

INT: And what--

HB: At an age of 55-- or 54, excuse me.

INT: And what happened after that to your mom and--

HB: Well, Mom stayed in Auburn where we had eventually moved. And I tried to work in the local rope shop as a laborer, hoping to move up the ladder. And eventually, I could see that it was not going to happen. And also, I looked around the town. And if you weren't a merchant or if you weren't a professional, you were destined to stay there. In my opinion, I don't want to say anything negative, not what I wanted to do. So I decided to come to New York. And in New York, I had several positions as sales-- as a salesperson and went on the road for one company selling cake ornaments. And then I got a job selling bridal gowns. And then I got a job-- and I lasted a pretty good-- I did very well in both positions. But then I got a job that selling for a distributor of DuPont materials. And I-- we're selling plastic and did very well and for about eight years. And then on-- somehow when I met my wife, my father-in-law was just going into business. And he convinced me that there's only one thing to do is have your own business. So I went in with him, Phil Young, one of the nicest persons anyone would ever meet. And Phil and I built a nice business. And he-- and I continue it on till this day.

INT: Great. And who did you marry? And how did you meet?

HB: I-- well, when my mother moved to New York, we shared an apartment for a length of time on West End Avenue. And then from there, we moved to Pinehurst Avenue in Manhattan. That's upper Manhattan. And in the elevator, I saw these two nice ladies, young girls. And I said, yeah, I need to get a date with one of them. But I didn't know which one. They were sisters. So I asked the one out. And we've been together for 60-- what-- 65, 66 years now.

INT: And what is her name? And when was she born and where?

HB: Her name is Lois. Her maiden name was Young, and she still is. And she was born in May 13 of '36 in the Bronx.

INT: And did she have an occupation or--

HB: Well, she was going to Hunter College at the time. So it was I think, one of the reasons she married me, so I could drive her to school every day from-- but, no, kidding. [PAUSES] She ended up-- she took as a interviewer for the state of New York, interviewing-- getting people jobs, an employment interviewer. And she did very well there. She did get a teaching degree, but decided that she'd rather do this other thing.

INT: And did you have children?

HB: Yes, we have Amy, our oldest. And she's married to Charles, and Douglas, who's wife's name is Kim. Kim and Doug live in Greer, South Carolina. And Amy and Charles live up in Park Ridge, New Jersey.

INT: And her last name is--

HB: Koebler.

INT: And your grandchildren?

HB: I have three grandchildren. And the names are Olivia, who is the youngest. She's 15. Noah is the oldest. He's 24. And let's say Lucas is 22.

INT: And in your life, what kind of organizations have you belonged to-- or things, activities you've done?

HB: Well, I became very active in Spring Valley, in their town, in the village. I was, you know, in with the PTA, I got involved with that. That led me eventually, because of the things I was doing, to become a member of the Board of Education there for 13 years. I think five of them were-- I was the president. It was one of the most interesting parts of my life because I have a point where I want to give back. The country has been so good to me. I want to do something for the country. I still do. I still frustrated at this age, I don't know how to give back any more because nobody wants a 92-year-old guy. They won't hire you. They-- even as a volunteer, they-- you're too old to volunteer. So I don't feel that way. I still feel good. But some of the other organizations-- I was with the B'nai B'rith. I got awards there. I was involved with the first Earth Day that this country had. And I, not single handedly, but mostly because myself and four other people got interested in Earth Day at the time. And we got-- the district had a trailer, which was sitting abandoned. And we equipped that so it would become Earthmobile. And each one of the 15 elementary schools contributed displays. And the trailer was taken from one school to another for a year. In that-- doing that ecology thing, I became interested in it, very interested in the recycling. And I reached out to a number of people, and the Marcal Paper Goods Company agreed to pick up our schoolroom waste. And that went on for a couple of years. But recycling is a peculiar thing, if you get orange peels or apple cores in there, they can't use it. And there was enough of that. So that program had to be discontinued. But it was very successful. And as a result of both those two things, the district made a proposal to the US government. And we were given what was a large track of land which used to be army barracks. And it was called the Nike site. And the district was given that to develop an ecology park. So I take some pride in having been a part of that. After my school-- or during my school board venture, our son became very interested in soccer. And so we went to Yankee Stadium, where the New York News, I believe, was bringing in PelÃ©. He was a big star at that time. And so we went to that. And when we were there, I was sitting next to some people from Asbury Park. And we were talking. And they asked me, do we have soccer? And I said-- and where we live-- I said, no. So when we went back, I talked to the board, recreation board for the town and asked if we could start one. They said, well, yes, we can if you'll do it. So he said, we'll give you-- we'll take care of your insurance and we'll take care of supplies for the first year. So that was July 4th. And by September, we had 380 kids. We had uniforms. We had insurance. We had balls. And we had coaches. Anybody that had a foreign accent automatically became a coach because the people here didn't know that much about soccer. But if you were a foreigner coming in, you knew soccer. So we did that. I stayed with that for, I don't know, 8 or 10 years. It seems like it. And they honored me by naming the soccer league in my name, which-- and we gave out-- every year, we gave out $1,000 to-- each to three different high schools. And we gave them to seniors graduating who were going on to college. So that was pretty nice, too. From there, our town was looking to-- where I lived, they were looking to form a village. And so they asked me to come and help form the village. And I became a founding father of the village of Chestnut Ridge, New York, and so proud of that, too. But the soccer thing has followed me forever. [PAUSES] Five years ago, we were looking at a house in Florida. And the lady says, are you Hal Block? And I said, yes. Well, this is our son. He's looking for a house here. And he got a scholarship, by the way, from your soccer league. So every once in a while, I'll run into someone that still remembers the soccer league. It since then has been taken in by another league, and they became even larger. But my whole thing in the soccer leagues was to give kids fun. I was not as competitive as so many others. We had a men's team. We had a kosher team. And that was a pretty-- lot of fun for me, that.

INT: Did you play soccer too?

HB: I have never played soccer. And I don't think I've ever seen a full game of soccer because the games were played on a Sunday, I believe. And we had 13 fields. And I-- it was my responsibility to go to those 13 fields, make sure the referees got paid, make sure the referees showed up, make sure the games got started. So I don't think I ever-- I saw segments of every game in 13 different times on a Sunday. Yeah.

INT: Great. Was there a scholarship fund with handicapped?

HB: No. The handicap thing, I was chairman of the Ellen Gilman Scholarship Fund for Handicapped Children. Yes. Yeah. Yeah. The other organization that I should mention is my professional organization, which is Manufacturers Agents for the Food Service Industry, called MAFSI. They're national. I worked my way through-- they had local chapters. And I became president of that. You know, we work your way through the ranks. And I became eventually president of that. And then I became president of the national organization. So I got some very, very good friends in MAFSI. I still do. I still-- I'm still a member. So that that that was certainly a wonderful time that I had with and having with MAFSI.

INT: So many positive things in your life, which is great. When did you learn what happened in the Holocaust?

HB: When did I learn what happened to the Holocaust? I would-- followed World War II as a child, in high school. And what I did was I had a big atlas. And as the United States and the Allies became victorious, I would color in the areas that they did. I don't think that-- in those days, I don't think people talked about the Holocaust. I don't recall the word Holocaust in those days. People were being killed. But I can't say that in those days during the war-- no, I don't think-- I don't think we as a country realized and I think it was kept from us intentionally until after the war, until those concentration camps got opened up and we saw the horrible things that these Nazis did.

INT: How did you feel when you saw that?

HB: I felt ashamed. I felt-- I felt ashamed of being a German and that the Germans did this to fellow Germans. I don't think-- I think everyone has a prejudice bone-- I don't think-- in their body. I don't think I do. But every once in a while I say, hey, you're prejudiced. It's good that you realize your prejudice no matter what. I don't care if it's vanilla or chocolate ice cream, but your prejudiced about something.

INT: So you felt you kind of were German at the same time you-- how did you feel about German and German products or going back there?

HB: I don't buy German products. I try not to buy German products. I won't buy German products knowingly. I still have a little sense of it. I think it's sort of, you know, really shouldn't hold that out anymore. But then I see what's going on in the world. And is it starting all over again? Maybe it's just a different area. Maybe it's also still in Germany. It sure is. I'm sure it is. I do remember-- I think when I went to parts of New York, I felt very comfortable. When I went to some other parts, I didn't feel as comfortable being a Jew.

INT: When was that?

HB: Oh, that was even into the '70s and '80s.

INT: Were you ever back in Germany?

HB: No, I wouldn't go back. I was offered a free trip to go back. And, no-- I mean-- I mean, I was-- I shouldn't even talk. I was-- my car broke down, and my friend gave me-- lent me his Mercedes. I didn't want to buy-- I didn't want to drive it. Well, sure enough, my wife and I went. We picked the car up. We drove it. She wanted an espresso. We stopped off at the-- I picked up her drink, came back out, tried to start up the car, and it was dead. So I said, Hitler is after me. Next thing-- next time with that car, I had a flat tire late at night. Again, I blamed it on Hitler. I have not bought a German car. I think they're wonderful. I think they're wonderful engineers. I think they're very bright people. It's just a crazy thing with me. All right, you want to buy it, that's up to you.

INT: So a part of you felt German, but another part of you doesn't. You're a little angry at that, what they did?

HB: Yeah. And then when I see what's going on now. It hasn't gone away. It's still there. They haven't learned.

INT: In Germany or somewhere else?

HB: Yeah, in Germany. I mean, my best friend, her husband and she-- he was a president of AJC in New York. And their whole thing was bringing us together with Germany. And she and I always used to have these-- I says, I can't do it. But I could-- I saw, you know, they were trying to do the right thing. They were trying to repair. And I was not in the repair mood.

INT: Did you reunite with any family members after the war?

HB: We didn't have anyone left in Germany. So there was-- you know, they were all here. And so we did see our friends, our relatives in California, St. Louis, Chicago. All over the country, we were spread. Yeah.

INT: And how-- is there anything else you'd like to say about any of this?

HB: Well, what I'd like to say is people should really appreciate this country. It has been so good to myself and my family. It has-- you can make what you want. You're given the opportunity in this country. You have freedom. You don't know how precious it is. You have to really-- and you have to give to it, too. If you-- you should put some effort and you should work to make this country better. And I love this country. And the people have been so good to me. And people around here, they're just so good to me. I can't believe it. I can't-- I hear all this stuff going on around that people are trying to rip it down. And they should really look at themselves. And they should look at this country and say, look what I've got. I even have the opportunity to bitch. But you have to be able to give. You have to give to this country because it's-- we're the only country that's-- I don't know. I don't know what the words are now. But it's, to me, I guess it's God bless America.

INT: Do you feel related to Israel at all and what's going on?

HB: I am very upset about it. I have a friend who's got a lot of his family there. And [PAUSES] I-- of course, we are hoping-- we continue to support Israel in their fight. I mean, where people-- where a country gets attacked and then there are people who attacked that country for protecting themselves and trying to defend and keep their country intact, I don't understand that thinking.

INT: Have you been there?

HB: No. That's a regret that I have. And-- yeah.

INT: Any final words?

HB: No, I think I gave you my final words.

INT: Great. Thank you so much.

HB: Well, thank you.

CREW: One.

INT: And who are they?

HB: Yes, this is a picture of my mother and her family on the-- in this picture, I would guess that my mother is in her early, maybe 18 or so. The man on the right is my grandfather, Sigmund, my grandmother, Regina, my uncle, James, my uncle Ernest, and my uncle, Hans.

INT: Thank you. And who are they?

HB: This is my father's mother, Carolina, or Caroline, and my aunt Hilda. And she ended up moving, getting married, and living in Buffalo, New York.

INT: Thank you. Who are these people?

